# Manuscript PDF page 89

                            SA N TO D O M I NG O



   “Primero se come.” First you eat.
   “Abuela.”
   “¿Y tu mamá?” She put a second ladle in my bowl without asking.
“¿Todavía guarda la lata?”
   I stopped chewing. “How do you know about the can?”
   “Because I told her to start it.” She almost smiled. “Thirty-one years
ago. I said, Altagracia, a woman who leaves should keep a can.” She
watched my face. “She never came back. But she kept the can. Of
course she kept the can.”
   I ate the rest of the sancocho in silence. It was the best food I’ve ever
had, and I’ve said that about four meals now, and I stand by all of
them.
   After she cleared, she walked me down the hall to a small back
room, my mother’s from before, narrow bed, one window onto the
street. On the way, she passed a closet door, and she touched it. Just the
flat of two fingers against the wood, the way you touch a sleeping an‐
imal to make sure it’s breathing.
   I didn’t ask. I knew what was in there. I’d known since I was eight
and she caught my wrist.
   “Mañana,” she said. “Tonight you sleep. Tomorrow I show you what
they wrote down.”
   She was halfway down the hall when she stopped and turned
around.
   “No hay pan.”
   “I don’t need bread, Abuela.”
   “Tú no. Yo.” She was already digging in the pocket of her house‐
dress. She put two folded bills in my hand and closed my fingers over
them. “Pan de agua. Seis. Y una malta para mí. Across the street. And if
Cristian tells you cuarenta, you tell him I said treinta.”
   The colmado across the street had no door, just a wide mouth of
light with the grate rolled halfway down, shelves to the ceiling, a cooler
humming, a salami on a hook, plantains in a crate, and a counter be‐
hind iron bars like every colmado I’ve ever walked into in any country.
A hand-lettered sign over the register said NO SE FÍA. Out front, under
a bare bulb, four old men were playing dominoes on a folding table



